s://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/2400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fbd%2F21%2F5fdba23b482f80993cb68d032b90%2Ftulsaneontour-3.jpg 2400w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fbd%2F21%2F5fdba23b482f80993cb68d032b90%2Ftulsaneontour-3.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1350px) 953px, (min-width: 1025px) calc(100vw - 395px), (min-width: 768px) calc(100vw - 60px), calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fbd%2F21%2F5fdba23b482f80993cb68d032b90%2Ftulsaneontour-3.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fbd%2F21%2F5fdba23b482f80993cb68d032b90%2Ftulsaneontour-3.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fbd%2F21%2F5fdba23b482f80993cb68d032b90%2Ftulsaneontour-3.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fbd%2F21%2F5fdba23b482f80993cb68d032b90%2Ftulsaneontour-3.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fbd%2F21%2F5fdba23b482f80993cb68d032b90%2Ftulsaneontour-3.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fbd%2F21%2F5fdba23b482f80993cb68d032b90%2Ftulsaneontour-3.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fbd%2F21%2F5fdba23b482f80993cb68d032b90%2Ftulsaneontour-3.jpg 1800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/2400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fbd%2F21%2F5fdba23b482f80993cb68d032b90%2Ftulsaneontour-3.jpg 2400w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fbd%2F21%2F5fdba23b482f80993cb68d032b90%2Ftulsaneontour-3.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1350px) 953px, (min-width: 1025px) calc(100vw - 395px), (min-width: 768px) calc(100vw - 60px), calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/1100/quality/50/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fbd%2F21%2F5fdba23b482f80993cb68d032b90%2Ftulsaneontour-3.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fbd%2F21%2F5fdba23b482f80993cb68d032b90%2Ftulsaneontour-3.jpg" class="img" alt="A neon sign that says &amp;quot;Tulsa 66&amp;quot; lights up at sunset." fetchpriority="high" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 A neon sign lights up at sunset in Tulsa, Okla.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Nick Oxford for NPR                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;button class="toggle-caption" aria-label=""&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;abbr title="Toggle Caption" class="toggle-caption__info"&gt;i&lt;/abbr&gt;              &lt;/button&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;p&gt;During its heyday, Route 66 became an iconic road trip, connecting the main streets of cities and small towns from Chicago to Los Angeles. And neon signs were the radiant jewels scattered along the roadside.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;The glowing glass lights were a fairly new thing when Route 66 opened in November 1926. Parisian Georges Claude had invented the idea of running electricity through tubing filled with neon gas in 1910. The first neon sign arrived in the U.S. in 1922, at a Packard car dealership, and locals were wonderstruck. Reportedly, gapers caused traffic jams.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;Claude sold franchises, including one to what is now Claude Neon Federal Signs, founded 100 years ago, just like Route 66. It's still in operation in Tulsa, and the &lt;a href="https://cnfsigns.com/#:~:text=Since%20our%20origins%20in%20the,install%20on%20a%20regional%20basis." target="_blank"&gt;company's website &lt;/a&gt;says it's the largest sign company in Oklahoma.&lt;/p&gt;       &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;Neon: beautiful and dangerous&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;p&gt;CNF Signs still has "neon benders" — artisans who heat glass tubes in a fire and bend the glass by hand to form shapes and letters, just as Claude did. One of them is Tom Wolski, who said he tells visitors to his studio to stand way back.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-153106" class="bucketwrap image x-large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 4500;         --source-height: 4500;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x4500+0+0/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7b%2F2b%2Fbd548a8e4248886a5b364b0735a1%2Ftulsa-neon-trio2.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x4500+0+0/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7b%2F2b%2Fbd548a8e4248886a5b364b0735a1%2Ftulsa-neon-trio2.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x4500+0+0/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7b%2F2b%2Fbd548a8e4248886a5b364b0735a1%2Ftulsa-neon-trio2.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x4500+0+0/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7b%2F2b%2Fbd548a8e4248886a5b364b0735a1%2Ftulsa-neon-trio2.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x4500+0+0/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7b%2F2b%2Fbd548a8e4248886a5b364b0735a1%2Ftulsa-neon-trio2.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x4500+0+0/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7b%2F2b%2Fbd548a8e4248886a5b364b0735a1%2Ftulsa-neon-trio2.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x4500+0+0/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7b%2F2b%2Fbd548a8e4248886a5b364b0735a1%2Ftulsa-neon-trio2.jpg 1800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x4500+0+0/resize/2400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7b%2F2b%2Fbd548a8e4248886a5b364b0735a1%2Ftulsa-neon-trio2.jpg 2400w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x4500+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7b%2F2b%2Fbd548a8e4248886a5b364b0735a1%2Ftulsa-neon-trio2.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1350px) 953px, (min-width: 1025px) calc(100vw - 395px), (min-width: 768px) calc(100vw - 60px), calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x4500+0+0/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7b%2F2b%2Fbd548a8e4248886a5b364b0735a1%2Ftulsa-neon-trio2.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x4500+0+0/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7b%2F2b%2Fbd548a8e4248886a5b364b0735a1%2Ftulsa-neon-trio2.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x4500+0+0/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7b%2F2b%2Fbd548a8e4248886a5b364b0735a1%2Ftulsa-neon-trio2.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x4500+0+0/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7b%2F2b%2Fbd548a8e4248886a5b364b0735a1%2Ftulsa-neon-trio2.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x4500+0+0/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7b%2F2b%2Fbd548a8e4248886a5b364b0735a1%2Ftulsa-neon-trio2.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x4500+0+0/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7b%2F2b%2Fbd548a8e4248886a5b364b0735a1%2Ftulsa-neon-trio2.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x4500+0+0/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7b%2F2b%2Fbd548a8e4248886a5b364b0735a1%2Ftulsa-neon-trio2.jpg 1800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x4500+0+0/resize/2400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7b%2F2b%2Fbd548a8e4248886a5b364b0735a1%2Ftulsa-neon-trio2.jpg 2400w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x4500+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7b%2F2b%2Fbd548a8e4248886a5b364b0735a1%2Ftulsa-neon-trio2.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1350px) 953px, (min-width: 1025px) calc(100vw - 395px), (min-width: 768px) calc(100vw - 60px), calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x4500+0+0/resize/1100/quality/50/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7b%2F2b%2Fbd548a8e4248886a5b364b0735a1%2Ftulsa-neon-trio2.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x4500+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7b%2F2b%2Fbd548a8e4248886a5b364b0735a1%2Ftulsa-neon-trio2.jpg" class="img" alt="Tom Wolski is photographed illuminated by a red neon rectangle. The photo on the lower left shows him heating two glass tubes to weld them together for a neon sign. The photo on the right shows an array of different colors of glass tubes for neon signs on a shelf." loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 &lt;strong&gt;Top:&lt;/strong&gt; Tom Wolski is one of the two full-time neon benders at CNF Signs in Tulsa. &lt;strong&gt;Left:&lt;/strong&gt; He heats two glass tubes to weld them together for a neon sign. &lt;strong&gt;Right: &lt;/strong&gt;An array of glass tubes for neon signs.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Nick Oxford for NPR                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;button class="toggle-caption" aria-label=""&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;abbr title="Toggle Caption" class="toggle-caption__info"&gt;i&lt;/abbr&gt;              &lt;/button&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;p&gt;"In this room you have extreme electricity. You have fire, you have phosphorus, you have mercury. It is kind of dangerous; you have to pay attention to what you're doing. God knows why I would want to do this, but I love it," Wolski said, laughing.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;He was once one of two full-time neon benders at CNF Signs, but fewer and fewer companies have commissioned them, because they're so expensive.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;Instead, many are turning to LEDs, which can &lt;em&gt;seem&lt;/em&gt; like neon. LEDS are less expensive to make and operate and are less dangerous. But Joe Kesterson, senior designer at CNF Signs, maintains that LEDs are not as beautiful.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-153069" class="bucketwrap image x-large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 4500;         --source-height: 3000;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F23%2F86%2F641f9b1441d383b0a590ed9e689b%2Ftulsaneontour-23.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F23%2F86%2F641f9b1441d383b0a590ed9e689b%2Ftulsaneontour-23.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F23%2F86%2F641f9b1441d383b0a590ed9e689b%2Ftulsaneontour-23.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F23%2F86%2F641f9b1441d383b0a590ed9e689b%2Ftulsaneontour-23.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F23%2F86%2F641f9b1441d383b0a590ed9e689b%2Ftulsaneontour-23.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F23%2F86%2F641f9b1441d383b0a590ed9e689b%2Ftulsaneontour-23.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F23%2F86%2F641f9b1441d383b0a590ed9e689b%2Ftulsaneontour-23.jpg 1800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/2400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F23%2F86%2F641f9b1441d383b0a590ed9e689b%2Ftulsaneontour-23.jpg 2400w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F23%2F86%2F641f9b1441d383b0a590ed9e689b%2Ftulsaneontour-23.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1350px) 953px, (min-width: 1025px) calc(100vw - 395px), (min-width: 768px) calc(100vw - 60px), calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F23%2F86%2F641f9b1441d383b0a590ed9e689b%2Ftulsaneontour-23.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F23%2F86%2F641f9b1441d383b0a590ed9e689b%2Ftulsaneontour-23.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F23%2F86%2F641f9b1441d383b0a590ed9e689b%2Ftulsaneontour-23.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F23%2F86%2F641f9b1441d383b0a590ed9e689b%2Ftulsaneontour-23.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F23%2F86%2F641f9b1441d383b0a590ed9e689b%2Ftulsaneontour-23.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F23%2F86%2F641f9b1441d383b0a590ed9e689b%2Ftulsaneontour-23.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F23%2F86%2F641f9b1441d383b0a590ed9e689b%2Ftulsaneontour-23.jpg 1800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/2400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F23%2F86%2F641f9b1441d383b0a590ed9e689b%2Ftulsaneontour-23.jpg 2400w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F23%2F86%2F641f9b1441d383b0a590ed9e689b%2Ftulsaneontour-23.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1350px) 953px, (min-width: 1025px) calc(100vw - 395px), (min-width: 768px) calc(100vw - 60px), calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/1100/quality/50/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F23%2F86%2F641f9b1441d383b0a590ed9e689b%2Ftulsaneontour-23.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F23%2F86%2F641f9b1441d383b0a590ed9e689b%2Ftulsaneontour-23.jpg" class="img" alt="Joe Kesterson looks up at a sign at the Route 66 Neon Sign Park during a tour of neon signs in Tulsa, Oklahoma, US, on Tuesday, Oct. 20, 2026. Nick Oxford for NPR" loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 Joe Kesterson looks up at a neon sign at the Route 66 Neon Sign Park during a tour of neon signs in Tulsa.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Nick Oxford for NPR                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;button class="toggle-caption" aria-label=""&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;abbr title="Toggle Caption" class="toggle-caption__info"&gt;i&lt;/abbr&gt;              &lt;/button&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;p&gt;Neon glows, like a candle, he explained. LED lights are brighter and sharper. &lt;/p&gt;   &lt;div id="resnx-s1-5928338-100" class="bucketwrap internallink insettwocolumn inset2col"&gt;                     &lt;!-- END CLASS="BUCKET IMG" --&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;     &lt;!-- END ID="RESNX-S1-5928338-100" CLASS="BUCKETWRAP INTERNALLINK INSETTWOCOLUMN INSET2COL " --&gt;    &lt;p&gt;It may be easy to mistake LED lights for neon lights, he added, but "once you see both of them together, you can see [the LEDs] have almost a harshness to the light."&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;An interesting fact: Neon lights themselves are not all neon gas.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;"Neon gas is red when it illuminates," Kesterson said. "And so anything that you see that's got a warm color is usually going to have neon in it. But cool colors are made using argon gas."&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-153062" class="bucketwrap image x-large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 4500;         --source-height: 4500;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x4500+0+0/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F58%2F7a%2Fa2af1cfb4b28a9ea9ce093374c5e%2Ftulsa-neon-trio.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x4500+0+0/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F58%2F7a%2Fa2af1cfb4b28a9ea9ce093374c5e%2Ftulsa-neon-trio.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x4500+0+0/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F58%2F7a%2Fa2af1cfb4b28a9ea9ce093374c5e%2Ftulsa-neon-trio.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x4500+0+0/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F58%2F7a%2Fa2af1cfb4b28a9ea9ce093374c5e%2Ftulsa-neon-trio.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x4500+0+0/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F58%2F7a%2Fa2af1cfb4b28a9ea9ce093374c5e%2Ftulsa-neon-trio.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x4500+0+0/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F58%2F7a%2Fa2af1cfb4b28a9ea9ce093374c5e%2Ftulsa-neon-trio.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x4500+0+0/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F58%2F7a%2Fa2af1cfb4b28a9ea9ce093374c5e%2Ftulsa-neon-trio.jpg 1800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x4500+0+0/resize/2400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F58%2F7a%2Fa2af1cfb4b28a9ea9ce093374c5e%2Ftulsa-neon-trio.jpg 2400w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x4500+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F58%2F7a%2Fa2af1cfb4b28a9ea9ce093374c5e%2Ftulsa-neon-trio.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1350px) 953px, (min-width: 1025px) calc(100vw - 395px), (min-width: 768px) calc(100vw - 60px), calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x4500+0+0/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F58%2F7a%2Fa2af1cfb4b28a9ea9ce093374c5e%2Ftulsa-neon-trio.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x4500+0+0/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F58%2F7a%2Fa2af1cfb4b28a9ea9ce093374c5e%2Ftulsa-neon-trio.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x4500+0+0/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F58%2F7a%2Fa2af1cfb4b28a9ea9ce093374c5e%2Ftulsa-neon-trio.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x4500+0+0/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F58%2F7a%2Fa2af1cfb4b28a9ea9ce093374c5e%2Ftulsa-neon-trio.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x4500+0+0/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F58%2F7a%2Fa2af1cfb4b28a9ea9ce093374c5e%2Ftulsa-neon-trio.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x4500+0+0/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F58%2F7a%2Fa2af1cfb4b28a9ea9ce093374c5e%2Ftulsa-neon-trio.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x4500+0+0/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F58%2F7a%2Fa2af1cfb4b28a9ea9ce093374c5e%2Ftulsa-neon-trio.jpg 1800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x4500+0+0/resize/2400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F58%2F7a%2Fa2af1cfb4b28a9ea9ce093374c5e%2Ftulsa-neon-trio.jpg 2400w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x4500+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F58%2F7a%2Fa2af1cfb4b28a9ea9ce093374c5e%2Ftulsa-neon-trio.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1350px) 953px, (min-width: 1025px) calc(100vw - 395px), (min-width: 768px) calc(100vw - 60px), calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x4500+0+0/resize/1100/quality/50/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F58%2F7a%2Fa2af1cfb4b28a9ea9ce093374c5e%2Ftulsa-neon-trio.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x4500+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F58%2F7a%2Fa2af1cfb4b28a9ea9ce093374c5e%2Ftulsa-neon-trio.jpg" class="img" alt="Three photos: Top left shows a close up of a red illuminated neon tube and the one on the right shows a blue tube in the CNF Signs shop. Below both photos is a collection of neon signage at night in Tulsa." loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 Neon gas is used in warm colors, but argon gas is used to make cool colors, according to Joe Kesterson, senior designer at CNF Signs.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Nick Oxford for NPR                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;button class="toggle-caption" aria-label=""&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;abbr title="Toggle Caption" class="toggle-caption__info"&gt;i&lt;/abbr&gt;              &lt;/button&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;p&gt;Plus a couple of beads of mercury, which keeps the gas's molecules unstable and energized.&lt;/p&gt;       &lt;aside id="mid-story-recommendations-mount-piano" class="mid-story-recommendations-mount-piano" aria-label="recommended stories"&gt;&lt;/aside&gt;    &lt;!-- END ID="MID-STORY-RECOMMENDATIONS-MOUNT-PIANO" CLASS="MID-STORY-RECOMMENDATIONS-MOUNT-PIANO" ARIA-LABEL="RECOMMENDED STORIES" --&gt;    &lt;div id="resnx-s1-5928338-101" class="bucketwrap internallink insettwocolumn inset2col"&gt;                     &lt;!-- END CLASS="BUCKET IMG" --&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;     &lt;!-- END ID="RESNX-S1-5928338-101" CLASS="BUCKETWRAP INTERNALLINK INSETTWOCOLUMN INSET2COL " --&gt;    &lt;p&gt;The argon-and-mercury combination interacts with a phosphor powder coating on the inside of some tubes, which makes them glow in a rainbow of colors.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;"And it never works like you think it would," Kesterson said. "It's not like we learned in school, where blue and yellow make green. If I put blue gas in a yellow piece of neon, a lot of times it's orange or some weird color that you would not expect."&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;From migrants to vacationers&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-153066" class="bucketwrap image x-large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 4500;         --source-height: 3000;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F98%2F93%2F3f20884c45cfaf33193df97073d0%2Ftulsaneontour-5.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F98%2F93%2F3f20884c45cfaf33193df97073d0%2Ftulsaneontour-5.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F98%2F93%2F3f20884c45cfaf33193df97073d0%2Ftulsaneontour-5.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F98%2F93%2F3f20884c45cfaf33193df97073d0%2Ftulsaneontour-5.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F98%2F93%2F3f20884c45cfaf33193df97073d0%2Ftulsaneontour-5.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F98%2F93%2F3f20884c45cfaf33193df97073d0%2Ftulsaneontour-5.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F98%2F93%2F3f20884c45cfaf33193df97073d0%2Ftulsaneontour-5.jpg 1800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/2400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F98%2F93%2F3f20884c45cfaf33193df97073d0%2Ftulsaneontour-5.jpg 2400w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F98%2F93%2F3f20884c45cfaf33193df97073d0%2Ftulsaneontour-5.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1350px) 953px, (min-width: 1025px) calc(100vw - 395px), (min-width: 768px) calc(100vw - 60px), calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F98%2F93%2F3f20884c45cfaf33193df97073d0%2Ftulsaneontour-5.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F98%2F93%2F3f20884c45cfaf33193df97073d0%2Ftulsaneontour-5.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F98%2F93%2F3f20884c45cfaf33193df97073d0%2Ftulsaneontour-5.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F98%2F93%2F3f20884c45cfaf33193df97073d0%2Ftulsaneontour-5.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F98%2F93%2F3f20884c45cfaf33193df97073d0%2Ftulsaneontour-5.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F98%2F93%2F3f20884c45cfaf33193df97073d0%2Ftulsaneontour-5.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F98%2F93%2F3f20884c45cfaf33193df97073d0%2Ftulsaneontour-5.jpg 1800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/2400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F98%2F93%2F3f20884c45cfaf33193df97073d0%2Ftulsaneontour-5.jpg 2400w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F98%2F93%2F3f20884c45cfaf33193df97073d0%2Ftulsaneontour-5.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1350px) 953px, (min-width: 1025px) calc(100vw - 395px), (min-width: 768px) calc(100vw - 60px), calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/1100/quality/50/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F98%2F93%2F3f20884c45cfaf33193df97073d0%2Ftulsaneontour-5.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F98%2F93%2F3f20884c45cfaf33193df97073d0%2Ftulsaneontour-5.jpg" class="img" alt="A neon sign beckons visitors to stay at the Desert Hills Motel along Route 66 in Tulsa, Oklahoma, US, on Tuesday, Oct. 20, 2026. Nick Oxford for NPR" loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 A neon sign beckons visitors to stay at the Desert Hills Motel along Route 66 in Tulsa.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Nick Oxford for NPR                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;button class="toggle-caption" aria-label=""&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;abbr title="Toggle Caption" class="toggle-caption__info"&gt;i&lt;/abbr&gt;              &lt;/button&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;p&gt;Those bright colors "are part of the mystique of Route 66," Kesterson said. "That's what you would have seen traveling as a child with your parents across the country."&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;div id="resnx-s1-5928338-102" class="bucketwrap internallink insettwocolumn inset2col"&gt;                     &lt;!-- END CLASS="BUCKET IMG" --&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;     &lt;!-- END ID="RESNX-S1-5928338-102" CLASS="BUCKETWRAP INTERNALLINK INSETTWOCOLUMN INSET2COL " --&gt;    &lt;p&gt;Route 66 was the country's first paved highway and served as the primary route for migrating farmworkers during the Dust Bowl. Later, it became a military transport corridor during World War II. All that traffic led to gas stations, restaurants and motels, and those amenities led to vacationers.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;"And as you're flying down that highway, you need something that is going to stop that business and bring them to your doorstep," said Kathleen Franz, the associate director for curatorial affairs at the National Museum of American History and a specialist in the history of technology.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;"Businesses that had neon seemed up-to-date, modern, new. Here's a business willing to invest in itself."&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-153063" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 4500;         --source-height: 3000;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F27%2Fc8%2F41ae555f4f83a70bdb7fdea279d6%2Ftulsaneonfactory-1.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F27%2Fc8%2F41ae555f4f83a70bdb7fdea279d6%2Ftulsaneonfactory-1.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F27%2Fc8%2F41ae555f4f83a70bdb7fdea279d6%2Ftulsaneonfactory-1.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F27%2Fc8%2F41ae555f4f83a70bdb7fdea279d6%2Ftulsaneonfactory-1.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F27%2Fc8%2F41ae555f4f83a70bdb7fdea279d6%2Ftulsaneonfactory-1.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F27%2Fc8%2F41ae555f4f83a70bdb7fdea279d6%2Ftulsaneonfactory-1.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F27%2Fc8%2F41ae555f4f83a70bdb7fdea279d6%2Ftulsaneonfactory-1.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F27%2Fc8%2F41ae555f4f83a70bdb7fdea279d6%2Ftulsaneonfactory-1.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F27%2Fc8%2F41ae555f4f83a70bdb7fdea279d6%2Ftulsaneonfactory-1.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F27%2Fc8%2F41ae555f4f83a70bdb7fdea279d6%2Ftulsaneonfactory-1.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F27%2Fc8%2F41ae555f4f83a70bdb7fdea279d6%2Ftulsaneonfactory-1.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F27%2Fc8%2F41ae555f4f83a70bdb7fdea279d6%2Ftulsaneonfactory-1.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F27%2Fc8%2F41ae555f4f83a70bdb7fdea279d6%2Ftulsaneonfactory-1.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F27%2Fc8%2F41ae555f4f83a70bdb7fdea279d6%2Ftulsaneonfactory-1.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F27%2Fc8%2F41ae555f4f83a70bdb7fdea279d6%2Ftulsaneonfactory-1.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F27%2Fc8%2F41ae555f4f83a70bdb7fdea279d6%2Ftulsaneonfactory-1.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/1100/quality/50/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F27%2Fc8%2F41ae555f4f83a70bdb7fdea279d6%2Ftulsaneonfactory-1.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F27%2Fc8%2F41ae555f4f83a70bdb7fdea279d6%2Ftulsaneonfactory-1.jpg" class="img" alt="A vintage sign design drawing for 7th Street Cafe in Augusta, Kan., is displayed in a book at CNF Signs." loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 A vintage sign design is displayed in a book at CNF Signs.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Nick Oxford for NPR                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;button class="toggle-caption" aria-label=""&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;abbr title="Toggle Caption" class="toggle-caption__info"&gt;i&lt;/abbr&gt;              &lt;/button&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;p&gt;And that, Franz said, signified to travelers that the motel or restaurant was clean and safe. Suddenly, neon was everywhere. And it was inventive.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;The 1950s brought &lt;a href="https://www.smithsonianmag.com/history/googie-architecture-of-the-space-age-122837470/" target="_blank"&gt;Googie-style&lt;/a&gt; signs with Space Age shapes: boomerangs and parabolas, stars and swirls. Companies learned how to animate their signs, to make flashing arrows and looping lassos.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;Tulsa was a place that went particularly big. The 1934 "Meadow Gold" sign, a local landmark advertising a dairy, is the size of a school bus, with letters up to 7 feet high. It was originally built by Claude Neon Federal Signs, and they restored it about 70 years later.&lt;/p&gt;       &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;A decline and rebirth&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-153067" class="bucketwrap image x-large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 4500;         --source-height: 3000;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd6%2F8c%2F93b62ab3407f93348d57ffe12e25%2Ftulsaneontour-20.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd6%2F8c%2F93b62ab3407f93348d57ffe12e25%2Ftulsaneontour-20.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd6%2F8c%2F93b62ab3407f93348d57ffe12e25%2Ftulsaneontour-20.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd6%2F8c%2F93b62ab3407f93348d57ffe12e25%2Ftulsaneontour-20.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd6%2F8c%2F93b62ab3407f93348d57ffe12e25%2Ftulsaneontour-20.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd6%2F8c%2F93b62ab3407f93348d57ffe12e25%2Ftulsaneontour-20.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd6%2F8c%2F93b62ab3407f93348d57ffe12e25%2Ftulsaneontour-20.jpg 1800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/2400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd6%2F8c%2F93b62ab3407f93348d57ffe12e25%2Ftulsaneontour-20.jpg 2400w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd6%2F8c%2F93b62ab3407f93348d57ffe12e25%2Ftulsaneontour-20.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1350px) 953px, (min-width: 1025px) calc(100vw - 395px), (min-width: 768px) calc(100vw - 60px), calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd6%2F8c%2F93b62ab3407f93348d57ffe12e25%2Ftulsaneontour-20.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd6%2F8c%2F93b62ab3407f93348d57ffe12e25%2Ftulsaneontour-20.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd6%2F8c%2F93b62ab3407f93348d57ffe12e25%2Ftulsaneontour-20.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd6%2F8c%2F93b62ab3407f93348d57ffe12e25%2Ftulsaneontour-20.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd6%2F8c%2F93b62ab3407f93348d57ffe12e25%2Ftulsaneontour-20.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd6%2F8c%2F93b62ab3407f93348d57ffe12e25%2Ftulsaneontour-20.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd6%2F8c%2F93b62ab3407f93348d57ffe12e25%2Ftulsaneontour-20.jpg 1800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/2400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd6%2F8c%2F93b62ab3407f93348d57ffe12e25%2Ftulsaneontour-20.jpg 2400w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd6%2F8c%2F93b62ab3407f93348d57ffe12e25%2Ftulsaneontour-20.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1350px) 953px, (min-width: 1025px) calc(100vw - 395px), (min-width: 768px) calc(100vw - 60px), calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/1100/quality/50/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd6%2F8c%2F93b62ab3407f93348d57ffe12e25%2Ftulsaneontour-20.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd6%2F8c%2F93b62ab3407f93348d57ffe12e25%2Ftulsaneontour-20.jpg" class="img" alt="The Meadow Gold neon sign along Route 66 lights up the night in Tulsa, Oklahoma, US, on Tuesday, Oct. 20, 2026. Nick Oxford for NPR" loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 The Meadow Gold neon sign along Route 66.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Nick Oxford for NPR                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;button class="toggle-caption" aria-label=""&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;abbr title="Toggle Caption" class="toggle-caption__info"&gt;i&lt;/abbr&gt;              &lt;/button&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;p&gt;Over time, five new interstate highways replaced portions of Route 66, and it stopped being recognized entirely as a federal highway in 1985. Small town traffic dwindled, and businesses closed. Neon signs are expensive to run, and many were turned off, sold, trashed or, like the Meadow Gold sign, stored.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;But almost immediately, a movement started to preserve and commemorate the road, and in 1999, a law authorized the &lt;a href="https://www.nps.gov/orgs/1453/rosi.htm" target="_blank"&gt;National Park Service Route 66 Corridor Preservation Program.&lt;/a&gt; Along the old route, communities hoping to draw back those tourists started restoring roadside attractions, including neon signs.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;In 2004, the Meadow Gold sign was saved when the building it was on was slated for demolition. CNF Signs rebuilt the scaffolding and restored the porcelain letters that house the neon; it was relit and placed in what is now the Meadow Gold section of town in 2009.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;In 2019, looking toward the Route 66 centennial, the Tulsa Route 66 Commission started a &lt;a href="https://www.cityoftulsa.org/neongrant" target="_blank"&gt;grant program&lt;/a&gt; for businesses that wanted to make new signs out of neon or LED tubing, or restore old ones. Now, there are more than 100 neon signs in Tulsa, including re-created vintage ones in Neon Sign Park, part of Cyrus Avery Plaza.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-153068" class="bucketwrap image x-large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 4500;         --source-height: 3000;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F26%2F3a%2F65c92caa4804bdbb363603a57db2%2Ftulsaneontour-22.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F26%2F3a%2F65c92caa4804bdbb363603a57db2%2Ftulsaneontour-22.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F26%2F3a%2F65c92caa4804bdbb363603a57db2%2Ftulsaneontour-22.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F26%2F3a%2F65c92caa4804bdbb363603a57db2%2Ftulsaneontour-22.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F26%2F3a%2F65c92caa4804bdbb363603a57db2%2Ftulsaneontour-22.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F26%2F3a%2F65c92caa4804bdbb363603a57db2%2Ftulsaneontour-22.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F26%2F3a%2F65c92caa4804bdbb363603a57db2%2Ftulsaneontour-22.jpg 1800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/2400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F26%2F3a%2F65c92caa4804bdbb363603a57db2%2Ftulsaneontour-22.jpg 2400w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F26%2F3a%2F65c92caa4804bdbb363603a57db2%2Ftulsaneontour-22.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1350px) 953px, (min-width: 1025px) calc(100vw - 395px), (min-width: 768px) calc(100vw - 60px), calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F26%2F3a%2F65c92caa4804bdbb363603a57db2%2Ftulsaneontour-22.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F26%2F3a%2F65c92caa4804bdbb363603a57db2%2Ftulsaneontour-22.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F26%2F3a%2F65c92caa4804bdbb363603a57db2%2Ftulsaneontour-22.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F26%2F3a%2F65c92caa4804bdbb363603a57db2%2Ftulsaneontour-22.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F26%2F3a%2F65c92caa4804bdbb363603a57db2%2Ftulsaneontour-22.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F26%2F3a%2F65c92caa4804bdbb363603a57db2%2Ftulsaneontour-22.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F26%2F3a%2F65c92caa4804bdbb363603a57db2%2Ftulsaneontour-22.jpg 1800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/2400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F26%2F3a%2F65c92caa4804bdbb363603a57db2%2Ftulsaneontour-22.jpg 2400w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F26%2F3a%2F65c92caa4804bdbb363603a57db2%2Ftulsaneontour-22.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1350px) 953px, (min-width: 1025px) calc(100vw - 395px), (min-width: 768px) calc(100vw - 60px), calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/1100/quality/50/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F26%2F3a%2F65c92caa4804bdbb363603a57db2%2Ftulsaneontour-22.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x3000+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F26%2F3a%2F65c92caa4804bdbb363603a57db2%2Ftulsaneontour-22.jpg" class="img" alt="Vintage neon signs light up the Route 66 Neon Sign Park in Tulsa, Oklahoma, US, on Tuesday, Oct. 20, 2026. Nick Oxford for NPR" loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 The Will Rogers Motor Court sign is on display at the Route 66 Neon Sign Park.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Nick Oxford for NPR                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;button class="toggle-caption" aria-label=""&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;abbr title="Toggle Caption" class="toggle-caption__info"&gt;i&lt;/abbr&gt;              &lt;/button&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;p&gt;Those replicas include signs otherwise lost to history, like the Will Rogers Motor Court, with a cowboy on a rearing horse (The name "Will Rogers" is circled by a gold lasso), and the Tulsa Auto Court, which features a red car with a driver's silhouette in the window and what seem to be moving tires.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;The park is on a very quiet section of Route 66, next to a river and train tracks and below the interstate. Above it, traffic whizzes by on I-244.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;You can't see the signs from the modern highway, but they're there, glowing.&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Audio and digital edited by Steven Drummond. Chloee Weiner mixed the audio. Emily Bogle edited the photos.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/div&gt;</description>
